// Pull request: #5956 new: Native Messaging Host Registration Detection for Windows, macOS and Linux
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/5956
// Pull request author: Fz0x00
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential Suspicious Browser Native Messaging Host Registration
// Sigma ID: a58b22c1-c860-4ff9-8091-fd6c605923c7
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: PersonalRules
// Date: 2026-04-23 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: persistence, T1176.001, execution
// Description: Detects the creation of a native messaging host registration via the Windows registry. Native
//   messaging allows browser extensions to communicate with local binaries via stdin/stdout, which
//   can be abused to execute arbitrary commands and escape the browser sandbox. Adversaries such as
//   ChromeAlone (DEF CON 33) register native messaging hosts to achieve shell execution from a
//   malicious extension.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate browser extensions installing native messaging hosts (password managers, screen readers, etc.)
//   - Enterprise software deployment tools installing browser extensions
//   - Browser updates that register new native messaging hosts
// References:
//   - https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/develop/concepts/native-messaging
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/extensions-chromium/developer-guide/native-messaging
//   - https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Mozilla/Add-ons/WebExtensions/Native_manifests
//   - https://github.com/praetorian-inc/ChromeAlone
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/32adff53abb9817be521584dba2c24fa19649564/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_win_browser_native_messaging_host.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.
// Note: 1 wildcard comparison(s) rewritten to 'matches regex' (KQL treats * in contains/endswith/== as a literal character)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey matches regex @"(?i)\\NativeMessagingHosts.*$"
